{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Then came Amalek and fought with Israel in Rephidim.</b> In the Yalkut it brings down that Amalek resembles a fly, etc. — a parable of a king who fenced a vineyard, etc. The explanation of the parable and its reasoning is along the lines of what we wrote above in the portion of Genesis on the verse ״לַפֶּתַח חַטָּאת רֹבֵץ״ (Genesis 4:7): our Rabbis of blessed memory (Berakhot 61a) likened the evil impulse to a fly, whose mouth is too weak to bore a hole in whole flesh. But in a place where the fly finds some wound or sore, where the flesh is already somewhat open, there it settles and widens the breach. So too the evil impulse has no power to confront the perfectly righteous man in all his deeds, and no opening for sin and impurity has been opened to him at all; but one who comes to defile himself and makes openings for it — there it settles, to widen the breach until the violence of man rises up without remedy. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Yoma 39a): ״אַל תִּטַּמְּאוּ בְּכָל אֵלֶּה״ (Leviticus 18:24) — a man defiles himself a little, they defile him a lot, etc., and there we spoke at length about this matter, for there is its place." "So too Amalek: as long as Israel were whole with HaShem, and there was also peace among them, he could not prevail against them at all. And when he saw them in trial and contention — on account of the strife of the children of Israel, for there was quarreling among them; for all the encampments before the giving of the Torah were all in grumbling and dissension, as Rashi explained in the portion of Yitro (Exodus 19:2) — and ״וְעַל נַסּוֹתָם אֶת ה׳״, that they were contending even with HaShem — then Amalek found an open opening and a source opened for sin and impurity, and ״וַיָּבֹא עֲמָלֵק״ to add impurity upon their impurity by means of lying with males, as it is written (Deuteronomy 25:18) ״אֲשֶׁר קָרְךָ בַּדֶּרֶךְ״. And all this he did in order that HaShem might depart from them entirely, for His holiness, blessed be He, abides only in a place where the boundary of immorality is intact. And on account of this he could wage war against them, for then their hands were slack from everything: they had no salvation by way of miracle, for HaShem had departed from them, and they had no natural salvation either, that each man might help the other, for their hearts were divided, one from the other. Therefore it says ״וַיִּקְרָא שֵׁם הַמָּקוֹם הַהוּא מַסָּה וּמְרִיבָה עַל רִיב בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וְעַל נַסֹּתָם אֶת ה׳ הֲיֵשׁ ה׳ בְּקִרְבֵּנוּ אִם אָיִן״. And immediately ״וַיָּבֹא עֲמָלֵק וַיִּלָּחֶם עִם יִשְׂרָאֵל בִּרְפִידִים״, for ״רְפִידִים״ is the letters of ״פְּרִידִים״, and the letters of ״רָף יָדַיִם״, and the one depends on the other: for by means of these two kinds of separation — that they separated from HaShem and the division of hearts among themselves — they came to slackness of hands. And for this reason the people had no water in Rephidim, because they slackened their hands from the Torah, which is likened to water; therefore they lacked plain water. And water was not given to them except from the rock in Horeb, the place of the receiving of the Torah. And for this reason they likened Amalek to a fly, for he could do nothing to Israel as long as they did not open an opening to him. And this is what they said: a parable of a king who fenced a vineyard and said, 'Whoever breaches the fence, the dog will bite him,' etc. — meaning that by breaching the fence and making a breach in it, he thereby opens an opening for the dog so that it can enter there. So too Amalek came and engaged Israel because they themselves had breached their fence. And likewise Haman, who came from the seed of Amalek, came against Israel with these arguments, saying (Esther 3:8) ״יֶשְׁנוֹ עַם אֶחָד מְפֻזָּר וּמְפֹרָד״ — that is, scattered, each man from his fellow, and separated from the Shekhinah." "And perhaps there is an allusion in the slackness of hands — that they slackened their hands from the Torah, which was given from hand to hand, and from prayer, which is through the lifting of hands, and from the trait of lovingkindness, a thing given from hand to hand. And a proof for this is from what is written, ״וְאַהֲרֹן וְחוּר תָּמְכוּ בְיָדָיו״ (Exodus 17:12), in order to uphold these three pillars upon which the house of Israel stands, and upon them three shepherds upheld them: Moses, Aaron, and Miriam, in whose merit HaShem gave them manna, the well, and the clouds of glory. Manna in the merit of Moses, for the Torah was given only to eaters of the manna; and the well that supplied all their needs in the merit of Miriam, who used to provide food for the little children, as it says ״וַתְּחַיֶּיןָ אֶת הַיְלָדִים״ (Exodus 1:17); and clouds of glory in the merit of Aaron, who was occupied with the sacrificial service, which caused the Shekhinah to dwell in Israel, ״וַעֲנַן ה׳ עַל הַמִּשְׁכָּן״ (Numbers 9:18). And when Amalek heard of the death of Aaron, that the clouds of glory had departed, then he came to wage war against them, as is found in the portion of Hukkat (Numbers 21:1). And from there is a proof that the merit of Aaron stood for Israel to save them from Amalek, who is a strap of rebelliousness against Israel forever. Therefore in this war Moses wanted his hands to be faithful, by his calling to mind for them the merit of these three pillars, and consequently Moses stood in the middle with the merit of receiving the Torah in his hand, and Aaron and Hur supported his hands from this side and from that — for Aaron had the merit of divine service in his hand, and Hur, Miriam's son, had the merit of lovingkindness in his hand."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:10.605353348Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}